                <h2>R/s Analysis to estimate the Hurst&nbsp;exponent</h2>

                <p>R/s analysis (or Rescaled Range analysis) was originally=
 devised by Harold Edwin Hurst in its studies of the Nile discharge in 1951=
. It is a rather simple method, easily implemented in a program and that pr=
ovides a direct estimation of the Hurst Exponent which is a precious indica=
tor of the state of randomness of a time-series. It is especially interesti=
ng in revealing the existence of long-term dependence, which prevents, when=
 it exists, the time-series to be reasonably modelised by a random walk.</p=
>
<p>I wish to expose here the minimal information for understanding the meth=
od, I will also provide a few references for those who wish to deepen their=
 understanding of the matter.</p>
<p>Given a time-series with n elements <img src=3D"https://s0.wp.com/latex.=
php?latex=3DX_1%2C+X_2%2C...%2CX_n+&amp;bg=3De6e6e6&amp;fg=3D333333&amp;s=
=3D0&amp;c=3D20201002" alt=3D"X_1, X_2,...,X_n " class=3D"latex">, the R/s =
statistic is defined as:</p>
<p><img src=3D"https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=3DR%2Fs%28n%29%3D+%5Cfrac%=
7B1%7D%7Bs%7D%5BMax_k%7B%5Csum_%7Bi%3D1%7D%5E%7Bk%7D%28x_i-%5Cbar%7Bx%7D%29=
%7D-Min_k%7B%5Csum_%7Bi%3D1%7D%5E%7Bk%7D%28x_i-%5Cbar%7Bx%7D%29%7D%5D&amp;b=
g=3De6e6e6&amp;fg=3D333333&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D20201002" alt=3D"R/s(n)=3D \fr=
ac{1}{s}[Max_k{\sum_{i=3D1}^{k}(x_i-\bar{x})}-Min_k{\sum_{i=3D1}^{k}(x_i-\b=
ar{x})}]" class=3D"latex"></p>
<p>Where <img src=3D"https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=3D1%5Cleq+k+%5Cleq+n=
%2C+&amp;bg=3De6e6e6&amp;fg=3D333333&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D20201002" alt=3D"1\l=
eq k \leq n, " class=3D"latex"><br>
<img src=3D"https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=3D%5Cbar%7Bx%7D&amp;bg=3De6e6=
e6&amp;fg=3D333333&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D20201002" alt=3D"\bar{x}" class=3D"lat=
ex"> is the arithmetic mean<br>
And <img src=3D"https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=3Ds%3D%5Csqrt%7B%5Cfrac%7=
B1%7D%7Bn%7D%5Csum%28x_i-%5Cbar%7Bx%7D%29%5E2%7D+&amp;bg=3De6e6e6&amp;fg=3D=
333333&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D20201002" alt=3D"s=3D\sqrt{\frac{1}{n}\sum(x_i-\ba=
r{x})^2} " class=3D"latex"> is the standard deviation from the mean.</p>
<p>With this R/s value, Hurst found a generalization of a result found by E=
instein in 1905 (<a href=3D"http://www.the-nucleus.org/document/ServeFile.c=
fm?ID=3D2146&amp;DocID=3D5">Investigations on the Theory of the Brownian Mo=
vement</a> ) as equation (11) (in the cited paper) in the following formula=
:</p>
<p><img src=3D"https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=3DE%5BR%2Fs%28n%29%5D%3DCn=
%5EH+%5Ctext%7B+as+%7Dn%5Crightarrow%5Cinfty+%5C%2C%5C%2C%5C%2C%5C%2C%281%2=
9&amp;bg=3De6e6e6&amp;fg=3D333333&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D20201002" alt=3D"E[R/s(=
n)]=3DCn^H \text{ as }n\rightarrow\infty \,\,\,\,(1)" class=3D"latex"></p>
<p>Where H is the Hurst exponent.<br>
From there, it is clear that we can obtain an estimation of the Hurst expon=
ent pretty easily from an R/s analysis.<br>
Several sites and articles propose a detailed methodology to implement R/s =
Analysis, I primarily use the approach exposed in a paper by O. Rose from F=
ebruary 1996:<br>
<a href=3D"http://www.eecis.udel.edu/~mills/fractal/tr137.pdf">Estimation o=
f the Hurst Parameter of Long-Range Dependent Time Series </a><br>
With a slight difference, however, I shall only plot one value of R/s for e=
ach value of d, in a manner similar to the following site: <a href=3D"http:=
//www.bearcave.com/misl/misl_tech/wavelets/hurst/index.html#RSEstimation">E=
stimating the Hurst Exponent</a><br>
Anyway, I feel both articles are not very clear in their notations, and I t=
herefore will detail the analysis I wish to implement.</p>
<p><strong>I- RESCALED RANGE ANALYSIS</strong></p>
<p>Considering the time series above <img src=3D"https://s0.wp.com/latex.ph=
p?latex=3DX_j+%28j%3D1%2C..%2CN%29&amp;bg=3De6e6e6&amp;fg=3D333333&amp;s=3D=
0&amp;c=3D20201002" alt=3D"X_j (j=3D1,..,N)" class=3D"latex"><br>
We divide the time series into <img src=3D"https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?late=
x=3DK%5Eu+&amp;bg=3De6e6e6&amp;fg=3D333333&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D20201002" alt=
=3D"K^u " class=3D"latex">(*) non-overlapping blocks of length <img src=3D"=
https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=3Dd%5Eu%3D%5Cfrac%7BN%7D%7BK%5Eu%7D+&amp;=
bg=3De6e6e6&amp;fg=3D333333&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D20201002" alt=3D"d^u=3D\frac{=
N}{K^u} " class=3D"latex"><br>
And we fix:  <img src=3D"https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=3Dt_i%3Dd%5Eu%28=
i-1%29%2B1+&amp;bg=3De6e6e6&amp;fg=3D333333&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D20201002" alt=
=3D"t_i=3Dd^u(i-1)+1 " class=3D"latex"></p>
<p>Next we get a new time series <img src=3D"https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?la=
tex=3DW%28i%2Ck%29%3A+&amp;bg=3De6e6e6&amp;fg=3D333333&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D20=
201002" alt=3D"W(i,k): " class=3D"latex"></p>
<p><img src=3D"https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=3DW%28i%2Ck%29%3D%5Csum_%7=
Bj%3D1%7D%5E%7Bk%7D%7B%5BX_%7Bt_i%2Bj-1%7D-%5Cfrac%7B1%7D%7Bd%5Eu%7D%5Csum_=
%7Bv%3D1%7D%5E%7Bd%5Eu%7D%7BX_%7Bt_i%2Bv-1%7D%7D%5D%7D%2C%5C%2Ck%3D1%2C..%2=
Cd%5Eu+&amp;bg=3De6e6e6&amp;fg=3D333333&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D20201002" alt=3D"=
W(i,k)=3D\sum_{j=3D1}^{k}{[X_{t_i+j-1}-\frac{1}{d^u}\sum_{v=3D1}^{d^u}{X_{t=
_i+v-1}}]},\,k=3D1,..,d^u " class=3D"latex"></p>
<p>From there, we get the following rescaled range:<br>
<img src=3D"https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=3DR%2Fs%28i%2Cu%29%3D%5Cfrac%=
7BR%28i%2Cd%5Eu%29%7D%7Bs%28i%2Cd%5Eu%29%7D+&amp;bg=3De6e6e6&amp;fg=3D33333=
3&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D20201002" alt=3D"R/s(i,u)=3D\frac{R(i,d^u)}{s(i,d^u)} "=
 class=3D"latex"></p>
<p>With:</p>
<p><img src=3D"https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=3DR%28i%2Cd%5Eu%29%3DMax%5=
C%7B0%2CW%28i%2C1%29%2C...%2CW%28i%2Cd%5Eu%29%5C%7D-Min%5C%7B0%2CW%28i%2C1%=
29%2C...%2CW%28i%2Cd%5Eu%29%5C%7D&amp;bg=3De6e6e6&amp;fg=3D333333&amp;s=3D0=
&amp;c=3D20201002" alt=3D"R(i,d^u)=3DMax\{0,W(i,1),...,W(i,d^u)\}-Min\{0,W(=
i,1),...,W(i,d^u)\}" class=3D"latex"><br>
And<br>
<img src=3D"https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=3Ds%28i%2Cd%5Eu%29%3D%5Csqrt%=
7B%5Cfrac%7B1%7D%7Bd%5Eu%7D%5Csum_%7Bj%3D1%7D%5E%7Bd%5Eu%7D%5BX_%7Bt_i%2Bj-=
1%7D-%5Cfrac%7B1%7D%7Bd%5Eu%7D%5Csum_%7Bv%3D1%7D%5E%7Bd%5Eu%7DX_%7Bt_i%2Bv-=
1%7D%5D%5E2%7D&amp;bg=3De6e6e6&amp;fg=3D333333&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D20201002" =
alt=3D"s(i,d^u)=3D\sqrt{\frac{1}{d^u}\sum_{j=3D1}^{d^u}[X_{t_i+j-1}-\frac{1=
}{d^u}\sum_{v=3D1}^{d^u}X_{t_i+v-1}]^2}" class=3D"latex"></p>
<p>Taking the mean over <img src=3D"https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=3Di+&=
amp;bg=3De6e6e6&amp;fg=3D333333&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D20201002" alt=3D"i " clas=
s=3D"latex">, we then get <img src=3D"https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=3DR=
%2Fs%28d%5Eu%29&amp;bg=3De6e6e6&amp;fg=3D333333&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D20201002"=
 alt=3D"R/s(d^u)" class=3D"latex">:</p>
<p><img src=3D"https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=3DR%2Fs%28d%5Eu%29%3D%5Cfr=
ac%7B1%7D%7BK%5Eu%7D%5Csum_%7Bi%3D1%7D%5E%7BK%5Eu%7DR%2Fs%28i%2Cd%5Eu%29&am=
p;bg=3De6e6e6&amp;fg=3D333333&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D20201002" alt=3D"R/s(d^u)=
=3D\frac{1}{K^u}\sum_{i=3D1}^{K^u}R/s(i,d^u)" class=3D"latex"></p>
<p>Considering equation (1):&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp=
;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;        <img src=3D"https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=3D=
log%28R%2Fs%28d%5Eu%29%29%3Dlog%28C%29+%2BHlog%28d%5Eu%29&amp;bg=3De6e6e6&a=
mp;fg=3D333333&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D20201002" alt=3D"log(R/s(d^u))=3Dlog(C) +H=
log(d^u)" class=3D"latex"></p>
<p>We can plot <img src=3D"https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=3Dlog%28R%2Fs%=
28d%5Eu%29%29+&amp;bg=3De6e6e6&amp;fg=3D333333&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D20201002" =
alt=3D"log(R/s(d^u)) " class=3D"latex"> vs <img src=3D"https://s0.wp.com/la=
tex.php?latex=3Dlog%28d%5Eu%29+&amp;bg=3De6e6e6&amp;fg=3D333333&amp;s=3D0&a=
mp;c=3D20201002" alt=3D"log(d^u) " class=3D"latex"> for u varying, <img src=
=3D"https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=3DH+&amp;bg=3De6e6e6&amp;fg=3D333333&=
amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D20201002" alt=3D"H " class=3D"latex"> is then the slope o=
f the regression line which we simply get from the linear least squares met=
hod.<br>
Fixing:<br>
<img src=3D"https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=3Dx_u%3Dlog%28d%5Eu%29+&amp;b=
g=3De6e6e6&amp;fg=3D333333&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D20201002" alt=3D"x_u=3Dlog(d^u=
) " class=3D"latex"> and <img src=3D"https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=3Dy_=
u%3Dlog%28R%2Fs%28d%5Eu%29%29+&amp;bg=3De6e6e6&amp;fg=3D333333&amp;s=3D0&am=
p;c=3D20201002" alt=3D"y_u=3Dlog(R/s(d^u)) " class=3D"latex"></p>
<p>We get:</p>
<p><img src=3D"https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=3D%5Cboxed%7BH%3D%5Cfrac%7=
BU%5Csum_%7Bu%7Dx_uy_u+-%28%5Csum_%7Bu%7Dx_u%29%28%5Csum_%7Bu%7Dy_u%29%7D%7=
BU%28%5Csum_%7Bu%7Dx_%7Bu%7D%5E2%29-%28%5Csum_%7Bu%7Dx_u%29%5E2%7D%7D+%5Cte=
xt%7B+with+%7Du+%5Ctext%7B+varying+from+%7D1+%5Ctext%7B+to+%7D+U+&amp;bg=3D=
e6e6e6&amp;fg=3D333333&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D20201002" alt=3D"\boxed{H=3D\frac{=
U\sum_{u}x_uy_u -(\sum_{u}x_u)(\sum_{u}y_u)}{U(\sum_{u}x_{u}^2)-(\sum_{u}x_=
u)^2}} \text{ with }u \text{ varying from }1 \text{ to } U " class=3D"latex=
"></p>
<p>(*): <img src=3D"https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=3DN+&amp;bg=3De6e6e6&=
amp;fg=3D333333&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D20201002" alt=3D"N " class=3D"latex"> and=
 <img src=3D"https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=3DK%5Eu+&amp;bg=3De6e6e6&amp=
;fg=3D333333&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D20201002" alt=3D"K^u " class=3D"latex"> are =
chosen adequately so that <img src=3D"https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=3Dd=
%5Eu+&amp;bg=3De6e6e6&amp;fg=3D333333&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D20201002" alt=3D"d^=
u " class=3D"latex"> is always an integer.</p>
<p><strong>II- IMPLEMENTATION</strong></p>
<p>I implemented this method within an indicator for Metatrader 4 in order =
to compute a Fractalised Simple Moving Average for FOREX fluctuations. The =
implementation is not very interesting, partly because the computation time=
 on this platform are not very good, I was therefore constraint to use the =
R/s method on a very limited number of data.<br>
Anyway, the implementation can be seen on my other blog: <a href=3D"http://=
fractalfinance.blogspot.com/2009/10/rescaled-range-analysis.html">Rescaled =
Range Analysis</a></p>
